const scaleLabel = { high: 'Scalable', mid: 'Scalable with a business layer', low: 'Not so scalable' };
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best career options after B.Tech are the ones you can enter with real proof before your money or your family's patience runs out.</strong>
    For any branch, the choices are a job, an M.Tech or GATE and PSU route, an MBA, UPSC and other exams, an MS abroad, your own business, freelance or consulting work, or a switch into data and software. Picking by branch label or salary screenshot is the expensive mistake. Picking by CGPA, college signal, money and time, then building a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>, is how you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities and move earlier financial freedom closer.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ten routes are open to a B.Tech graduate. Lead with the ones where your skill can be sold directly, because they have the most room to grow. Keep the exam routes, but add a second plan next to them.</li>
      <li>Decide with four filters: CGPA and college signal, your branch, how much money you can risk, and how much time you have. Spend only about 10 percent of your education budget on a college, and avoid loans unless the programme is genuinely top for your subject.</li>
      <li>Take the next step this week: pick two routes, build one small piece of proof in each, and show it to three people who work in that area.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide covers every branch. If you want the detail for your own branch, jump to the
    <a href="#branch">branch guides</a>. More decisions of this kind sit in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a>, and a one-to-one conversation about your own numbers is available through
    <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-after-engineering/">career counselling after engineering</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="market">What you are walking into: B.Tech career opportunities in 2026</h2>

  <p>
    Four years of engineering gave you problem-solving, numbers and a technical vocabulary. It did not give you a ready route. The market now pays for skill you can show, and three numbers explain why.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    {stats.map((item) => (
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-card__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="stat-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: India Skills Report 2026 as reported by Careers360; Xpheno and Mint figures as reported by Outlook Business; Inc42 reporting a commerce ministry statement of April 2026. Check the originals before quoting them.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Skills first, degree second. For most of these routes the degree is the ticket in, not the reason you get paid. It matters most where it is a stated requirement: GATE, ESE and M.Tech all need the engineering degree. Everywhere else, a visible skill portfolio matters more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="routes">Career options after B.Tech: ten routes, led by real headroom</h2>

  <p>
    The routes below are ordered by how much income and reach can realistically grow, not by prestige or exam difficulty. Skill-led routes come first because you set the pace and the price. Stable, rank-based routes come last because pay is fixed and few people reach the top. Every route is labelled, and the crowded or risky ones stay on the list with their risk shown.
  </p>

  <div class="route-grid">
    {routes.map((r) => (
      <article class="route-card" id={`route-${r.n}`}>
        <div class="route-card__top">
