        <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span> {r.watch}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>Growth move</span> {r.grow}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path to notice:</strong> freelance, consulting or your own service business scales because you own the price, you can hire or systemise later, and repeat clients keep paying without a new hunt. It is not for everyone, and it works best after you have proof, but it is the route with no fixed ceiling.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Defence technical entry, PhD and research sit beside routes 07, 09 and 10. They are real options with small intakes and long ladders, so treat them the same way: one serious plan and one parallel skill. See the full
    <a href="/blog/career-options/engineering-career-options/">engineering career options</a> guide for the live exam calendar.
  </p>

  <h2 id="filters">Four filters before you pick: CGPA, college, money and time</h2>

  <p>
    Two people with the same branch can have very different best routes. Use your own numbers on this table. It shows which doors stay open and which need extra care.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Your situation</th>
          <th>Doors that stay open</th>
          <th>Needs extra proof or care</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doors.map((d) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Your situation"><strong>{d.situation}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Doors that stay open">{d.open}</td>
            <td data-label="Needs extra proof or care">{d.care}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    College tier is a small part of career success, often 1 to 10 percent by our estimate, and it shrinks as your proof grows. A top college helps with peers, recruiters and brand. It does not replace a skill you can show. To judge what a college can really do for you, read its
    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-read-college-placement-report-india/">placement report</a> for the denominator and the median, not only the highest offer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Money rules: college cost, MBA and MS maths, and debt</h2>

  <p>
    You already spent four years and a large part of your family's budget on the first degree. Before a second one, run the numbers. The rules below are planning heuristics, not laws.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The 10 percent rule</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Start with about 10 percent of your family's total education budget as the college spend. On a Rs 10 lakh budget, that is about Rs 1 lakh.</li>
        <li>The rest funds living security, tools, books, projects, internships and later upskilling.</li>
        <li>Above roughly 20 percent needs course-specific evidence: a necessary credential, verified placement outcomes, and a realistic income after the full cost.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The debt caution</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Avoid education debt unless a credential is required or the programme is genuinely top for your subject.</li>
        <li>If you can repay it after a job, ask whether the skills you built, not the degree, did the work. Then why borrow?</li>
        <li>Information is free or cheap. Compare the paid route with YouTube, official documents and good low-cost courses first, and judge each course on its own.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>MBA payback, worked out</h3>

  <p>
    These round numbers are only to show the method. They are not a fee quote or a salary promise.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>Add the full cost.</strong> Suppose fees are Rs 20 lakh and you give up Rs 4 lakh a year of pay for two years. The cost is Rs 28 lakh.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimate the extra yearly pay honestly.</strong> If the MBA lifts your pay by Rs 6 lakh a year over what you would have earned anyway, payback is about 4.7 years before tax and interest. If the lift is Rs 3 lakh, it is about 9 years.</li>
    <li><strong>Use real placement data.</strong> Use the median outcome of your programme, not the top offer, and ask alumni one question: what changed for you in year three?</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>MS abroad: the study-abroad lens</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Loan arithmetic.</strong> A Rs 40 lakh loan at 10 percent is Rs 4 lakh a year in interest before you repay any principal. Lender comparison sites quote everything from about 9 percent to above 13 percent depending on lender and collateral, so check live offers.</li>
    <li><strong>Exchange-rate risk.</strong> You borrow in rupees and pay tuition in foreign currency. If the rupee weakens by 5 percent, each foreign bill costs 5 percent more rupees.</li>
    <li><strong>Visa risk.</strong> Admission is one hurdle. The visa and a job are two more. IDP reported Indian student interest in the US falling more than 46 percent in a year.</li>
    <li><strong>Scholarships.</strong> A hoped-for scholarship is not funding. Check renewal terms, living costs, work limits and a plan for any gap.</li>
    <li><strong>The strict screen.</strong> Consider a large loan only for a genuinely top programme in your subject, and use "top five" as a strict screening rule, not a promise of success. Compare it with a debt-light route and test it against your family's real security.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For the full comparisons, read
    <a href="/blog/study-abroad/is-ms-in-usa-worth-it-india/">is an MS in the USA worth it</a>,
    <a href="/blog/study-abroad/education-loan-for-study-abroad-india/">education loan for study abroad</a>,
    <a href="/blog/study-abroad/ms-in-computer-science-vs-mba-india/">MS in computer science versus MBA</a> and
    <a href="/blog/career-options/best-mba-specialization-for-engineers/">the best MBA specialisation for engineers</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Keep a backup skill and a backup income path on every expensive or prestige-heavy route. A strong skill portfolio is what makes a degree pay back, not the other way round.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="two-stage">Routes that filter you twice</h2>

  <p>
    Many routes have a hard entry stage and a separate hard stage after it. Clearing the first does not guarantee the second. Count both before you commit years.
  </p>

